A||||12||fiero56||Where do you apt dwellers work on your car???||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-21-2001||11:12 PM||||Been doing the apartment deal this year, and my garage still hasn't been completed(getting closer). I miss having a garage. My apartment community has a no vehicle cleaning, maintenance, repair policy, so I've been using empty parking lots, side streets, and working in front of my apt under cover of darkness. I've picked out a parking lot to replace my front pads tommorrow, but wondering what other that refuse to allown anyone else to touch their car do without a place to work.||||reg||1
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||09-21-2001||11:25 PM||||My apt. complex has similar rules about not working on cars here. I either work on it at Formula88's apartment (which has no such rules) or out at my folks' house in the yard(where most of the tools are anyway). <P>When doing clutch swaps, I've done them in either the borrowed shop owned by a generous and helpful friend, or Formula88's parents' carport.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Loki||09-21-2001||11:31 PM||||Depending on how big the job is, ill just do it in the apt anyway   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">   <BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>http://members.home.net/fieroloki||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tonker||09-22-2001||12:39 AM||||My parent's garage.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The live about 20 mins away so it works out well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||09-22-2001||12:39 AM||||I will come to this problem when I graduate this December.  I plan to bite the bullet and rent a garage.  Working on cars is a stress reliever and even if it costs me a few hundred  a month it will be worth it.  I am not sure about a storage facility or a random shop, but something with power and water at minimum.  <P>My other issue is a car hauler.  I have access to three flatbeds and one tow truck now when I move away from good ol' Pa I really need to get looking for a long term truck for all types of work.  Pretty made up my mind to a International flatbed (rollback)  with a 19 or 20' bed with wheel-lift .  Not sure to go with a 7.3(444 or 444E) or 466(466E) as the engine.  But I plan on looking about 15G or so under 300K on the clock of the truck.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||09-22-2001||09:46 AM||||I have a big paved parking lot. I dont mind people working there as long as they stay away from customer cars, pick up their mess (including spills), and very important-DONT MAKE A BUNCH OF LOUD NOISE. That includes yelling and having a group party or loud music, and "I" determine whats loud. Break my rules and I call the cops. It cost me a fortune to pave and if im generous enough to let you there, give me respect. Most people would probably feel the same, and if you ask first would help.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||09-22-2001||09:07 PM||||My mom and dad live 15 miles from my apartment, so that's where I take the Fiero when it's time for service.  We did the steering rack there last week.<P>Small jobs like oil changes, headlight rebuild, etc., I do in the apartment parking lot.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||09-22-2001||09:40 PM||||I pulled into one of the unfinished garages today and did the front brakes(no one was nearby so I didn't have an audience <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">). I checked out the rears, but they still have issues. Brand new reman calipers and they are smoking my pads. I'm ordering a second set tommorrow(this is one of those times having two cars is nice), but two means twice as much $$$.||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||09-24-2001||10:31 AM||||I live in apts that have a no 'nothin' policy. But there is a Mall right beside us. If it isn't too big a job (sensor, interior work, etc) I do it in their parking lot, out near the road, where I won't bother anyone. A bigger job, I drive about 5 miles and do it at my buddy's house. He has an old Mercedes diesel sedan that leaks diesel and oil, so I never make as big a mess on the pavement as his car does just sitting there.<BR>As I've gotten older, I take the Fiero to a professional shop more and more, just too much going on in my life to spend time on repairs, as much as I enjoy it. Relaxing work, most of the time <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroLisa||09-24-2001||10:36 AM||||Well depends on where I am. 95% of the time at Roger's... Lowered the car at Brian Hoopers (had to find someone that knew what they were doing!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) Buuut this summer I did work on my car at my apt, even though there was a rule there too... Put it on jackstands in broad daylight and no one said anyting..whew!!! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Nashco||09-24-2001||02:00 PM||||I have the same rule saying you're not supposed to do anything with your car in the parking lot...but I, uh, forgot about that rule <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I had my car other car on jackstands in the parking lot for a month without anybody saying anything.  I just make sure to be clean and not spill any fluids, not make any loud noises or anything, and stay away from any good parking spots.  Nobody really seems to mind, and some people even come ask me what I'm doing, and how it's going.  It's really not that big of a deal...if you're apartments have a rule, just say you forgot and consider that your warning; you can do it until somebody complains, if nobody complains, you're alright.  If they do, you know better from then on <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||09-24-2001||07:04 PM||||I went out and bought a house for that very reason.  Now I have a yard, a garage, lots of space for more fieros...but I have a mortage as well and no Fiero money.  <P>This did not work out as planned......<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>--Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000012||EdsB52||09-25-2001||12:23 AM||||I used the cover of darkness when I used to live in apartments. I had a storage unit with a towable cherry picker. I would throw the new motor in the back of my truck, hook up the cherry picker, and do it before dawn. 15 years ago before I had all that stuff, I would go to the covered parking section at midnight and throw a come-along over the support truss and do the entire job in an evening. I did a Ford 400 one time. necessity is the mother of invention.||||reg||1
